========================================================================================================
2.  THE PURE-MODULE MULTI-PRIME IDENTITY
========================================================================================================
  pure module Li_j(x/s), s=1/lambda_2:  varsigma_p = v_p(lambda_2), so
  sum_p varsigma_p log p = log|lambda_2|  exactly, and
  [archimedean ceiling] + [full multi-prime gain at weight 1] = log(256/|l2|) + log|l2| = log 256.
  host                          l2     ceil  sum v_p log p    total  gam(u=7,m=14)     entry    margin
  Apery/CDT/X1(5)                1   5.5452         0.0000   5.5452        +0.0000   +1.3097    +6.491
  Catalan lvl 8/16               4   4.1589         1.3863   5.5452        +0.2546   +0.1780    -7.966
  Zagier F                       8   3.4657         2.0794   5.5452        +0.3819   -0.3878   -15.195
  Domb                           4   4.1589         1.3863   5.5452        +0.2546   +0.1780    -7.966
  Cooper s_18                   12   3.0603         2.4849   5.5452        +0.4564   -0.7188   -19.423
  sqrt(s_18)                    24   2.3671         3.1781   5.5452        +0.5837   -1.2846   -26.652
  sqrt(s_10)                     8   3.4657         2.0794   5.5452        +0.3819   -0.3878   -15.195
  => the multi-prime total is log 256 = 5.5452 on every host; but the p-adic half enters
     the entry condition at weight (u-1)^2/m^2 = 0.18367 and the archimedean half at weight 1,
     so lambda_2 = 1 (the Apery-perfect hosts) is optimal and every prime factor of lambda_2
     costs (1 - (u-1)^2/m^2) v_p log p = 0.8163 per v_p(lambda_2) log p.

